    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d600">

                <pb facs="0782" n="774" sameAs="frus:frus1941v04/pg774"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">894.24/1306</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum of Conversation, by the Adviser on Political
                        Relations</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Hornbeck</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-01-06">January 6, 1941</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Mr. Butler<anchor n="3" xml:id="d600-f1"/> and Mr. Hill<anchor n="4"
                        xml:id="d600-f2"/> called on me this afternoon. Mr. Butler referred to a
                    memorandum which he had left with me under date November 20, 1940<anchor n="5"
                        xml:id="d600-f3"/> on the subject of possibly cutting down on Japan’s piling
                    up of petroleum reserves by taking tankers out of operation into Japanese ports.
                    He referred to previous conversations on that subject and the fact that thus far
                    this Government has given no official expression of its views. He then gave me
                    the memorandum and compilation of figures that are here attached<anchor n="6"
                        xml:id="d600-f4"/> and asked Mr. Hill to summarize the contents and purport
                    thereof. Mr. Hill elaborated <pb facs="0783" n="775" xml:id="pg775"/> somewhat
                    on the subject of the problem of withholding increments of reserve supplies from
                    Japan and spoke of the quotas which the Japanese Government has allotted to the
                    Standard-Vacuum and Shell oil companies for importation into and distribution in
                    Japan. He said that the British Government and Shell advocate non-acceptance by
                    the companies of those quotas but that Standard feels that the quotas should be
                    accepted. [Here follows a discussion of details.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>S[<hi rendition="#small-caps">tanley</hi>] K. H[<hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">ornbeck</hi>]</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
